[candidate · 9:57] Beans first.

Reason: beans are almost certainly the single largest input line for a specialty coffee chain, so if the 16-point COGS swing has one dominant cause, beans is the highest-prior candidate. Pulling it first either explains most of the gap, in which case I focus on sourcing and pricing, or it rules the hypothesis out, which is just as useful because it immediately redirects me to dairy, food, and waste.

It's also the input most exposed to a global commodity market, so it's the one that could plausibly move enough to shift COGS 16 points in two years without anyone at the client making a decision. Dairy and packaging move, but they're smaller and usually less volatile.

One thing I'd want alongside the share: whether the share moved because bean cost per pound rose, or because pounds used per transaction rose. Same share, very different problem — one is procurement, the other is operations.
