[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 17:10] I don't have competitor pricing. Let me hand you the cost structure summary.

Beyond pricing, what else could the client do to rebuild margin? Give me the full set.

[candidate · 18:40] First, note the trajectory: COGS rose 8 points, then 8 points again, and margin fell 24 to 15 to 6. Straight-line, next year is roughly minus 3%. This is urgent, not a watch item.

On levers beyond headline pricing, four groups.

Pricing refinements: selective increases on low-elasticity items like espresso drinks rather than across the board, resizing cups so the price-per-ounce moves without a visible price change, and bundling food with drinks to lift ticket.

Procurement: lock forward contracts or hedge beans, consolidate suppliers across 200 stores for volume terms, dual-source dairy and packaging, and reformulate — cheaper cup stock, alternative milk blends.

Mix: push customers toward structurally high-margin items, brewed coffee and espresso over milk-heavy and food items, through menu layout and promotions.

Cost structure: overhead drifted up 2 points on 15% revenue growth, which is the opposite of the leverage you'd expect — worth auditing. Also revisit lease terms and audit waste.

Pricing is the only lever big enough to close 18 points alone, though.
